    "https://forms.bethelks.edu/about/who-we-are/career-opportunities/current-position-openings/assistant-coach-track-and-field-throws": {
      "title": "Assistant Coach: Track and Field",
      "description": "Assistant Coach:  Track and Field - throws | Bethel College Forms System Skip to main content Menu Main navigation Home Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Coach:  Track and Field - throws This position will support the overall track and field program in all aspects of the coaching and administrative duties assigned to an assistant coach. Certifications, Licenses, Registrations Valid driver's license Classification Part-time; salaried (12-month position) Department Athletic Department Education and/or Experience Bachelor’s degree Previous High School or Club equivalent coaching experience (College level preferred) Essential Functions Support the Mission, Vision, & Values of Bethel College Help identify, evaluate, recruit and retain a roster of eligible student-athletes Assist in organizing practices and competitions Assist in administrative duties of the programs in regards to budget management, travel management, supervision of study hall and academic reports, coaching, scouting of opponents, recruiting, transportation and other daily operational needs. Demonstrate flexibility and willingness to learn and adapt to changing work processes, including moving from in-person to remote work and back, as necessitated by College policy or as directed by the supervisor Satisfy the duties of a Campus Security Authority (CSA) relating to Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (“Clery Act”) and satisfy the duties of an Official with Authority (OWA) and Mandated Reporter relating to Title IX and the Bethel College E qual Opportunity, H arassment, & Non-Discrimination Policy and Procedures Arrive on time for work, attend work regularly, and successfully complete the responsibilities of the position Comply with Bethel College policies and procedures Help build team culture that promotes Champions of Character goals Faculty or Staff Staff Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The essential functions of the position require: Minimal lifting (up to 25 lbs.) and reaching Moving about to accomplish tasks or moving from one facility or building location to another Ascending and descending stairs Ability to perform tasks both sitting and standing for extended periods of time Ability to function well in a standard office setting Outdoor work environment Medium/indoor work environment Primary Responsibilities All coaching staff assume the responsibility of instruction of their respective sport Teach and demonstrate techniques, tactics and strategies to help athletes improve and reach their full potential Provide constructive feedback and support the athletes to help them develop their skills and improve performance Develop and implement individualized training programs for athletes based on their abilities and goals Communicate effectively with athletes and other coaches to ensure a positive and supportive team environment Stay current with trends and developments in coaching and incorporate new techniques and strategies Provide input for student evaluation and grade assigned for sport Skills Required Proficient computer skills Effective leadership skills Strong written and verbal communication skills Strong team building/management skills Ability to collaboratively work in a team driven athletic department environment Ability to work across academic and administrative departments in a cooperative manner Work Environment and Conditions All the essential job functions are subject to reasonable accommodation. All job requirements listed indicate the minimum level of knowledge, skills and/or ability deemed necessary to perform the job proficiently. This job description is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements. Individuals may be required to perform other job-related instructions as requested by the supervisor, subject to reasonable accommodation. This position description is not all-inclusive and may change at the option of the (supervisor or department head). Any major change will result in the position being reviewed for reclassification. Application Information Please complete a Bethel College application and upload a cover letter, resume, and three references with contact information. Apply now Bethel College does not discriminate in its employment practices or in its educational programs or activities on basis of race, religion, color, sex/gender, pregnancy, political affiliation, religion, creed, ethnicity, national origin (including ancestry), citizenship status, physical or mental disability (including perceived disability), age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status (including disabled veteran, recently separated veteran, active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran, and Armed Forces Service Medal veteran), predisposing genetic characteristics, domestic violence victim status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state, or federal law. The College also prohibits retaliation against any person opposing discrimination or participating in any discrimination investigation or complaint process internally or externally. Reports of misconduct, questions regarding Title IX, and concerns about noncompliance should be directed to the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator). For a complete copy of the policy or for more information, please contact the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator) at titleixcoordinator@bethelks.edu or (316) 284-5248 or the Assistant Secretary of Education within the Office for Civil Rights (OCR). Both the applicable Bethel College Policy and further information is available online . Copyright © Bethel College. All rights reserved. Questions regarding Title IX should be referred to the Title IX Coordinator . Report a concern . Nondiscrimination Statement Title IX Information Privacy Policy Cares Act View All Policies"
    },
    "https://forms.bethelks.edu/about/who-we-are/career-opportunities/current-position-openings/director-information-and-media-services": {
      "title": "Director of Information and Media Services",
      "description": "Director of Information and Media Services | Bethel College Forms System Skip to main content Menu Main navigation Home Breadcrumb Home Jobs Director of Information and Media Services The Director of Information and Media Services (IMS) is Bethel College's senior technology leader, responsible for the strategy, security, and day-to-day operation of all campus technology services. Reporting to the Vice President for Business and Finance, the Director leads a small team of IT, web, audiovisual professionals and student employees, and serves as both a hands-on systems administrator and a strategic technology advisor to the Cabinet. The Director oversees the college's network, server, identity, and cloud infrastructure; directs the institutional information security program and serves as the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) Qualified Individual; and develops and communicates technology policy. The position manages technology budgets and vendor relationships, ensures compliance with GLBA, FERPA, and other applicable regulations, and works closely with students, faculty, and staff to deliver reliable, secure, and user-focused technology in support of Bethel's academic mission. Certifications, Licenses, Registrations No specific certification required at hire; however, demonstrated IT competency through industry-recognized certification is strongly preferred. Classification Full-time; Salaried Exempt Department Business and Finance Education and/or Experience Bachelor’s degree in an Information Technology related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum of five (5) years of progressive IT experience, including systems and/or network administration. Minimum of two (2) years of experience in a supervisory, manager, or director role, including demonstrated experience coaching and evaluating. Experience in higher education or a similar multi-constituency environment preferred. Essential Functions Leadership and Strategy Provide leadership and oversight for all technology services across the Bethel College campus, including infrastructure, user support, web, and audiovisual services. Work with Cabinet, faculty, and staff to define and execute a vision and multi-year plan for campus technology goals and projects. Develop and manage technology related budgets, including software licensing and contract renewals, equipment lifecycle replacement, and capital requests. Evaluate and manage relationships with technology vendors, consultants, and service providers; review proposed technology purchases and contracts for security, privacy, integration, and support impact before adoption. Plan and lead system rollouts, upgrades, and migrations, including stakeholder communication and user training. Information Security and Compliance Serve as the “qualified individual” for the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) in overseeing, implementing, and reporting to the Board on the information security program. Protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of institutional data, networks, and systems through layered security controls, including multi-factor authentication, endpoint detection and response, firewall and network segmentation, patch management, and least-privilege access. Conduct periodic IT risk assessments and oversee remediation of identified risks. Manage the identity and access lifecycle, including account provisioning and deprovisioning and periodic review of privileged and system access. Oversee security awareness training for employees and students. Ensure compliance with GLBA, FERPA, PCI DSS (where applicable), other data protection regulations, licensing agreements, and college technology policies. Technology Policy and Governance Develop, maintain, and communicate technology policies, standards, and procedures, including acceptable use, data classification and handling, and account security requirements. Lead the development and communication of institutional policy and guidance on artificial intelligence, including acceptable use, data privacy, and evaluation of AI tools. Identify, evaluate, and implement opportunities to integrate artificial intelligence tools and solutions that enhance operations and innovation across Bethel College. Communicate technology changes, planned maintenance, outages, and security notices clearly to the campus community. Infrastructure and Operations Primary responsibility for system and network administration of campus technology infrastructure, including servers, virtualization, storage, wired and wireless networks, firewalls, and cloud services. Administer identity and directory services, including Active Directory, Google Workspace, and single sign-on/multi-factor authentication platforms. Administer and troubleshoot remote access VPN services, including user provisioning, client support, and multi-factor authentication enforcement, as well as site-to-site VPN tunnels between campus and external networks. Manage SSL/TLS certificate, domain, and DNS renewals to prevent service interruptions. Build upon current documentation of IT systems, configurations, network diagrams, and standard operating procedures. User Support and Staff Development Manage the campus helpdesk system and ensure timely, high-quality support for students, faculty, and staff. Coordinate employee training for college-specific technology and processes. Provide supervision, mentorship, and performance oversight for staff and student employees. Faculty or Staff Staff Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions.  The essential functions of the position require: Ability to move freely around campus, including use of stairs. Moderate bending, crouching, lifting (up to 50 pounds). Dexterity as related to keyboard, telephone, and normal office equipment. Normal visual acuity. Working in front of computer monitor for extended periods of time (up to 8 hours). Operation of battery and small gas-powered vehicles. Occasional use of ladders and work in confined spaces such as network closets and above ceilings. Skills Required Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain technical concepts to non-technical audiences and write clear policy and documentation. Strong organizational, project management, and prioritization skills. Experience administering Microsoft Windows Server and Linux servers, including Active Directory (Group Policy, OUs, DNS, DHCP). Experience with virtualization (VMware vSphere or similar) and shared storage (Storage Area Network) environments. Experience managing enterprise networks, including switching, VLANs, routing, wireless, and network access control. Experience administering enterprise firewalls, including security policies, VPN, and high-availability configurations. Experience with identity and access management, including multi-factor authentication and single sign-on (SAML). Advanced administration of a cloud productivity platform (Google Workspace or Microsoft 365). Experience with enterprise backup and recovery, endpoint protection/EDR, and SSL/TLS certificate management. Working knowledge of information security frameworks and regulatory requirements (e.g., GLBA, FERPA, NIST). Knowledge of Microsoft Windows and macOS desktop operating system and prevalent desktop application software. Experience developing and managing budgets and vendor contracts. Strong problem-solving and troubleshooting skills. Skills Preferred: Cisco Duo administration, including Duo SSO, directory synchronization and authentication proxy. HPE Aruba Networking switching and wireless, and Aruba ClearPass (802.1X, RADIUS, NAC policy). Advanced Google Workspace administration using GAM and Google Cloud Directory Sync (GCDS). Fortinet FortiGate firewall administration (FortiOS upgrades, HA pairs, VPN, policy management). SentinelOne or comparable EDR platform administration and threat response. Veeam Backup & Replication, including backup job design, offsite/immutable copies, and restore testing. Experience with a centralized endpoint management platform, such as PDQ Deploy and PDQ Inventory, for software deployment, patching, and asset inventory. Storage Area Network (iSCSI/Fibre Channel) configuration and administration. Experience with higher education systems such as a student information system (e.g., Jenzabar) and learning management system (e.g., Canvas). Experience administering a helpdesk ticketing system. Supervisory Responsibility Supervise, evaluate, and train IMS staff and student employees for projects, day-to-day operations, and helpdesk support. Set clear expectations, conduct performance evaluations, and provide ongoing coaching and feedback. Address performance and conduct concerns through documentation, performance improvement plans, and progressive discipline. Develop onboarding and training for student employees with little or no prior IT experience. Work Environment and Conditions Indoor office environment, including movement between buildings on campus. This is a service and support position requiring a significant amount of contact with the campus community and the general public. Regular after-hours, weekend, and on-call work is required for scheduled maintenance, system outages, and security incidents. Periodic work in server rooms and network closets with elevated noise and temperature. All the essential job functions are subject to reasonable accommodation. All job requirements listed indicate the minimum level of knowledge, skills and/or ability deemed necessary to perform the job proficiently. This job description is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements. Individuals may be required to perform other job-related instructions as requested by the supervisor, subject to reasonable accommodation. This position description is not all-inclusive and may change at the option of the supervisor or department head. Any major change will result in the position being reviewed for reclassification. Application Information Please complete a Bethel College application and upload a cover letter, resume, and three references with contact information. Apply now Bethel College does not discriminate in its employment practices or in its educational programs or activities on basis of race, religion, color, sex/gender, pregnancy, political affiliation, religion, creed, ethnicity, national origin (including ancestry), citizenship status, physical or mental disability (including perceived disability), age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status (including disabled veteran, recently separated veteran, active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran, and Armed Forces Service Medal veteran), predisposing genetic characteristics, domestic violence victim status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state, or federal law. The College also prohibits retaliation against any person opposing discrimination or participating in any discrimination investigation or complaint process internally or externally. Reports of misconduct, questions regarding Title IX, and concerns about noncompliance should be directed to the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator). For a complete copy of the policy or for more information, please contact the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator) at titleixcoordinator@bethelks.edu or (316) 284-5248 or the Assistant Secretary of Education within the Office for Civil Rights (OCR). Both the applicable Bethel College Policy and further information is available online . Copyright © Bethel College. All rights reserved. Questions regarding Title IX should be referred to the Title IX Coordinator . Report a concern . Nondiscrimination Statement Title IX Information Privacy Policy Cares Act View All Policies"
    },
    "https://forms.bethelks.edu/about/who-we-are/career-opportunities/current-position-openings/assistant-professor-music-two-positions-one-full-time-and-one-three-quarters-time": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Music Two Positions - (One Full-Time and One Three-Quarters Time)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Music Two Positions - (One Full-Time and One Three-Quarters Time) | Bethel College Forms System Skip to main content Menu Main navigation Home Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Professor of Music Two Positions - (One Full-Time and One Three-Quarters Time) The Music Department is responsible for the effective development and delivery of the department's curriculum, advising of department majors, and providing quality instruction and student-centered learning experiences. The successful candidates will contribute to the continued development of the College's instrumental music program through teaching, conducting, student recruitment, and participation in departmental and College activities. Candidates should demonstrate strong musicianship, a commitment to undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts environment, and the ability to recruit talented music majors and non-majors and develop relationships with music educators throughout the region. The department seeks to fill two appointments beginning August 2027: one full-time tenure-track (1.0 FTE) position and one three-quarter-time (0.75 FTE) position. Responsibilities and areas of specialization will be shaped according to departmental needs and the strengths and qualifications of the successful candidates. Classification Two Positions: One Full-Time and One Three-Quarter Time Salaried / exempt Department Music Education and/or Experience MM or MMEd required DMA or Ph.D. in music preferred Demonstrated experience leading instrumental ensembles Qualifications to teach music education courses Essential Functions ● Support the mission, vision, and values of Bethel College ● Conduct instrumental ensembles, which may include the Wind Ensemble, Jazz Band, Chamber Orchestra, and Thresher Sound (athletic band) ● Teach courses that support the music major which may include conducting, instrumental methods and materials, and other courses based on departmental needs and candidate’s qualifications ● Teach applied instrumental lessons in the candidate’s area of specialization ● Instruction in other areas such as music technology, introduction to world music, music appreciation, first-year seminar, or other general education courses based on departmental needs and candidate’s qualifications ● Additional non-teaching duties include student recruiting (including visits to area schools and attendance at regional Music Educators Association (MEA) conferences), advising, participation in faculty governance, and supervising and mentoring students in the college’s Employment Experiences program ● Mandatory Reporter relating to Title IX and the Bethel College Equal Opportunity, Harassment, and Non-Discrimination Policy and Procedures ● Arrive on time for work, attend work regularly, and successfully complete the responsibilities of the position ● Comply with Bethel College policies and procedures Faculty or Staff Faculty Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The essential functions of the position require: Minimal lifting (up to 25 lbs.) and reaching Operate a computer and other office productivity machinery, such as telephone, calculator, copy machine, and printer for extended periods of time Moving about to accomplish tasks or moving from one classroom or building location to another Ascending and descending stairs Ability to perform tasks both sitting and standing for extended periods of time Ability to function well in a standard office setting Medium/indoor work environment Supervisory Responsibility Supervise and mentor students participating in the College's Employment Experiences program. Additional supervisory responsibilities may be assigned as appropriate to departmental needs. Work Environment and Conditions All the essential job functions are subject to reasonable accommodations. All job requirements listed indicate the minimum level of knowledge, skills and/or ability deemed necessary to perform the job proficiently. This job description is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements. Individuals may be required to perform other job-related instructions, as requested by the supervisor, subject to reasonable accommodation. This position description is not all-inclusive and may change at the option of the supervisor or department head). Any major change will result in the position being reviewed for reclassification. Application Information Please complete a Bethel College application and upload a cover letter, resume or curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, three references with contact information, and video links of rehearsals and performances.  In the cover letter, applicants should indicate their interest in the full-time and/or three-quarter-time appointment and describe their area of expertise and potential contributions to the Music Department. If you experience any difficulties uploading the performance links, please feel free to submit them directly to humanresources@bethelks.edu. Apply now Bethel College does not discriminate in its employment practices or in its educational programs or activities on basis of race, religion, color, sex/gender, pregnancy, political affiliation, religion, creed, ethnicity, national origin (including ancestry), citizenship status, physical or mental disability (including perceived disability), age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status (including disabled veteran, recently separated veteran, active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran, and Armed Forces Service Medal veteran), predisposing genetic characteristics, domestic violence victim status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state, or federal law. The College also prohibits retaliation against any person opposing discrimination or participating in any discrimination investigation or complaint process internally or externally. Reports of misconduct, questions regarding Title IX, and concerns about noncompliance should be directed to the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator). For a complete copy of the policy or for more information, please contact the Director of Human Resources & Compliance (Title IX Coordinator) at titleixcoordinator@bethelks.edu or (316) 284-5248 or the Assistant Secretary of Education within the Office for Civil Rights (OCR). Both the applicable Bethel College Policy and further information is available online . Copyright © Bethel College. All rights reserved. Questions regarding Title IX should be referred to the Title IX Coordinator . Report a concern . Nondiscrimination Statement Title IX Information Privacy Policy Cares Act View All Policies"
    }
  },
